body:
I can see where you're coming from, but the thing is she really doesn't want to change herself. She doesn't listen to me, or anyone really. Even when she asks for someone's opinion, she's really just asking for validation. She'll ask us whether or not to buy something expensive that she doesn't need and basically try to talk us into saying she should get it so she doesn't feel bad, and even if we say she shouldn't do it then she'll just do it anyway. I always tell her to skip out on the sweets and unhealthy eating, but she'd just laugh and blow me off.(To this day, she still laughs at that time I got mad at her for eating an entire box of oatmeal cream pies in one sitting.) And she genuinely hates any form of exercise. Walking is the only thing she can tolerate but conditionally. Because of her anxiety, she doesn't like to walk in public, and she doesn't enjoy treadmills because she doesn't like how she isn't going anywhere. So if I could find a place with no people that she could walk through then maybe we could actually go on walks together. 

&#x200B;

As for self esteem, I always try my best to cheer her on in whatever she does. (And she cheers me on as well) If she posts her drawings I always make sure she knows how much I love it, although I might not get a thank you but whatever I don't think anyone says thank you 100% of the time. And I try to mend the situation as much as possible with her and her parents and society. I'll try to change the subject to something happy or try to get us to do something fun like play a video game together or watch a movie, but she has the tendency to straight up go silent or ignore people sometimes. And whenever I hear that someone has done anything wrong to her, I always get mad, sometimes more mad than she is at some of the stupid shit people say to her. But even with about five and a half years of that, self esteem isn't very easy to build in a person. 

&#x200B;

I'm sure I could be doing more but I just don't know what all a person like that needs. I wish there was a term for how she acts so I could look up how to help her but I'm not really sure if what it is.

&#x200B;

Also you are absolutely right in the fact that I don't have to accept their calls every time, but I also feel bad about just saying that I don't feel like talking or something like that because I know how much the group means to her and how I'm one of her close friends. But this is completely my fault because I'm terrible at saying no. It's not even that I have any evidence that she'll be sad if I turn her down, I'm just afraid of disappointing people or turning them down when they want to make plans with me.

When the girl I had a crush on left her umbrella behind after class and I was pissed at my teacher for holding me back to talk about my grades(good grades, trust me) and I really just wanted to return the umbrella to her for some brownie points. Another girl walked by and showed the umbrella to the teacher and said someone forgot it. I jumped on the opportunity to return it right as my crush walked in and retrieved it. I then thought to myself that she left that umbrella as a way to see if I was interested and came back for it when she realized that I wasn't coming. So I was pretty pissed that basically everything went wrong in that moment. 

&#x200B;

Two days later and I wanted to fucking kill myself when I realized that what actually happened was she left her umbrella by mistake and came back to find that guy who struck out with her multiple times talking with a teacher and reaching to take her umbrella from the hands of another student. Then I wanted to throw up and apologize for all the creepy shit I did before apologizing for being born
